1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,138
First normal form is the agreement that a table is a table.

2
00:00:04,138 --> 00:00:07,263
Each cell holds one value, not a list.

3
00:00:07,263 --> 00:00:11,388
Each row is one thing, identified by a primary key.

4
00:00:11,388 --> 00:00:13,444
Each column has a single meaning.

5
00:00:13,444 --> 00:00:17,069
Look at a cell that says Harbor Light, Quiet Fields.

6
00:00:17,069 --> 00:00:20,541
That is two titles pretending to be one attribute.

7
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:22,347
You cannot sort by title.

8
00:00:22,347 --> 00:00:24,527
You cannot foreign-key to books.

9
00:00:24,527 --> 00:00:28,916
You cannot count how many titles Maya bought without splitting the string.

10
00:00:28,916 --> 00:00:30,847
Split into rows instead.

11
00:00:30,847 --> 00:00:32,611
One order line per book.

12
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:34,875
Now each title is atomic.

13
00:00:34,875 --> 00:00:41,444
Repeating groups are the same sin with extra columns: title1, title2, title3.

14
00:00:41,444 --> 00:00:46,652
title3 is empty for most rows, and a fourth book has nowhere to go.

15
00:00:46,652 --> 00:00:49,569
Rows scale; numbered columns do not.

16
00:00:49,569 --> 00:00:52,166
A primary key is part of the form.

17
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:56,375
Without a key, two identical rows are not two facts.

18
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:57,708
They are a smudge.

19
00:00:57,708 --> 00:00:59,638
Atomic does not mean short.

20
00:00:59,638 --> 00:01:02,833
A biography in TEXT can still be one value.

21
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:07,180
Atomic means you are not smuggling a second table into a cell.

22
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:11,902
JSON in a column can be a document, or it can be a hidden table.

23
00:01:11,902 --> 00:01:16,138
If you query inside it as if it had rows, you wanted rows.

24
00:01:16,138 --> 00:01:20,222
Names with a middle initial are still one name if that is how you treat them.

25
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:25,097
If you sort by last name, you wanted last_name as its own column.

26
00:01:25,097 --> 00:01:27,791
The test is use, not philosophy.

27
00:01:27,791 --> 00:01:30,555
Start with a messy grid from a spreadsheet.

28
00:01:30,555 --> 00:01:32,847
Circle every cell with a comma.

29
00:01:32,847 --> 00:01:35,277
Circle every title1 column.

30
00:01:35,277 --> 00:01:36,944
Those circles are the work.

31
00:01:36,944 --> 00:01:38,458
Make a child table.

32
00:01:38,458 --> 00:01:39,500
Give it a key.

33
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:40,847
Point at the parent.

34
00:01:40,847 --> 00:01:43,500
That is first normal form in practice.

35
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:45,902
Higher normal forms assume this one.

36
00:01:45,902 --> 00:01:49,833
If you skip it, later rules have nothing honest to stand on.

37
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:51,763
One cell, one value.

38
00:01:51,763 --> 00:01:53,291
One row, one thing.

39
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:55,041
A key that finds the row.

40
00:01:55,041 --> 00:01:57,013
Then you can talk about dependence.

41
00:01:57,013 --> 00:02:02,027
Repeating groups sometimes wear JSON or arrays in modern engines.

42
00:02:02,027 --> 00:02:05,236
An array of titles is still a list in a cell.

43
00:02:05,236 --> 00:02:09,638
If you query unnest, you wanted a child table for operational data.

44
00:02:09,638 --> 00:02:14,444
Document-looking payloads can stay documents if you always fetch them whole.

45
00:02:14,444 --> 00:02:18,375
The moment you join to a title inside, you wanted rows.

46
00:02:18,375 --> 00:02:22,555
CSV imports are the usual first-normal-form crime scene.

47
00:02:22,555 --> 00:02:25,777
Someone exported a spreadsheet with a list column.

48
00:02:25,777 --> 00:02:27,625
Split before you INSERT.

49
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:31,722
Or insert into a staging table and normalize in SQL.

50
00:02:31,722 --> 00:02:35,152
Multivalued dependencies are a later theory word.

51
00:02:35,152 --> 00:02:41,069
In practice you already felt them when two lists in one row multiplied into a mess.

52
00:02:41,069 --> 00:02:44,444
Keep lists out, and you will not need the word yet.

53
00:02:44,444 --> 00:02:48,708
Primary keys that are hashes of the whole row are a last resort.

54
00:02:48,708 --> 00:02:50,847
They say we could not find identity.

55
00:02:50,847 --> 00:02:52,152
Find identity.

56
00:02:52,152 --> 00:02:55,375
A line number plus parent id is identity.

57
00:02:55,375 --> 00:02:59,694
A random uuid is identity if you assign it at birth.

58
00:02:59,694 --> 00:03:01,597
A pile of titles is not.

59
00:03:01,597 --> 00:03:03,458
Walk a report you care about.

60
00:03:03,458 --> 00:03:06,861
If it needs COUNT of books, you need rows of books.

61
00:03:06,861 --> 00:03:13,944
If it needs a poster of the whole cart as one blob, you might keep a document copy, on purpose, later.

62
00:03:13,944 --> 00:03:17,875
First normal form is for the tables you query as tables.

63
00:03:17,875 --> 00:03:19,236
Keep them atomic.

64
00:03:19,236 --> 00:03:20,527
Give them keys.

65
00:03:20,527 --> 00:03:22,569
Then the later forms have a floor.

66
00:03:22,569 --> 00:03:26,125
If a report needs to count it, it needed its own row.

67
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:29,541
If a join needs to match it, it needed its own row.

68
00:03:29,541 --> 00:03:33,000
If a key needs to point at it, it needed its own row.

69
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,416
Lists in cells fail all three.

70
00:03:35,416 --> 00:03:37,347
Spreadsheets do not care.

71
00:03:37,347 --> 00:03:38,472
Engines do.

72
00:03:38,472 --> 00:03:40,222
Split, key, point.

73
00:03:40,222 --> 00:03:41,583
That is the repair.

74
00:03:41,583 --> 00:03:44,263
Do it before you load a million rows.

75
00:03:44,263 --> 00:03:48,680
Splitting a million lists is a worse afternoon than splitting twenty.

76
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,013
Atomic now, dependence later.

77
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:52,805
The floor has to exist.

78
00:03:52,805 --> 00:03:54,333
You are laying the floor.

79
00:03:54,333 --> 00:04:02,138
Normalization is putting each fact in one proper place so you can find it, change it once, and avoid anomalies.

80
00:04:02,138 --> 00:04:08,013
You start when you have a first ER draft: entities, attributes, relationships.

81
00:04:08,013 --> 00:04:10,263
Normal forms are nested rules.

82
00:04:10,263 --> 00:04:13,375
You cannot skip to third if first is still broken.

83
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:20,055
First normal form asks for a primary key, a type on each column, and atomic cells.

84
00:04:20,055 --> 00:04:26,597
Atomic means one value per intersection of row and column, not a list hiding in the cell.

85
00:04:26,597 --> 00:04:36,236
A phone column with 555-0100, 555-0199 is two facts in one seat.

86
00:04:36,236 --> 00:04:39,250
Queries cannot LIKE their way into integrity.

87
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:45,166
Split to a phones table, one number per row, with a type of mobile or desk if you need it.

88
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:49,865
A column of product ids separated by commas is the same crime.

89
00:04:49,865 --> 00:04:57,156
Repeating groups as extra columns — skill1, skill2, skill3 — are lists wearing hats.

90
00:04:57,156 --> 00:04:59,281
The fourth skill has nowhere to go.

91
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:02,753
The third skill is NULL for most people, forever.

92
00:05:02,753 --> 00:05:05,170
Make a skill assignment table instead.

93
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:09,531
Without a primary key, you cannot say which row you meant to update.

94
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:12,656
Two identical rows are not two facts.

95
00:05:12,656 --> 00:05:13,823
They are a dare.

96
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:18,170
Give every table a key before you talk about anything fancier.

97
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:23,767
Types belong in first normal form because an untyped column is a junk drawer.

98
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,962
The form is boring on purpose.

99
00:05:25,962 --> 00:05:29,698
Boring is what lets GROUP BY and foreign keys mean something.

100
00:05:29,698 --> 00:05:36,545
Fixing first normal form is splitting lists into rows, giving keys, and typing columns.

101
00:05:36,545 --> 00:05:39,115
It is not renaming tables for style.

102
00:05:39,115 --> 00:05:45,115
After first, you may still have redundancy across columns that depend on only part of a key.

103
00:05:45,115 --> 00:05:47,281
That is second normal form's job.

104
00:05:47,281 --> 00:05:51,378
Do not drag those problems into a first-normal-form discussion.

105
00:05:51,378 --> 00:05:56,156
One idea: one value, one type, one way to find the row.

106
00:05:56,156 --> 00:06:01,128
If a cell needs a comma to be honest, the table is not in first normal form.

107
00:06:01,128 --> 00:06:06,531
If two rows cannot be told apart, the table is not in first normal form.

108
00:06:06,531 --> 00:06:10,115
Repair those, then stop and look at the key's parts.

109
00:06:10,115 --> 00:06:19,726
Start with a spreadsheet of orders: one row, a customer, a cell of product names comma separated, a cell of quantities comma separated.

110
00:06:19,726 --> 00:06:22,990
That sheet is not a table in first normal form.

111
00:06:22,990 --> 00:06:29,128
Split products into order_lines, one product per row, one quantity per row.

112
00:06:29,128 --> 00:06:31,517
Give orders an order_id.

113
00:06:31,517 --> 00:06:35,962
Give lines a key of order_id plus line_no.

114
00:06:35,962 --> 00:06:40,837
Type quantity as integer, name as text, price as decimal.

115
00:06:40,837 --> 00:06:44,184
No repeating product1 product2 columns.

116
00:06:44,184 --> 00:06:46,976
Now GROUP BY and JOIN have a grain.

117
00:06:46,976 --> 00:06:52,378
Normalization started as library shelves: each book, one place.

118
00:06:52,378 --> 00:06:58,878
First normal form is the shelf labels and the rule against stuffing two books in one slot.

119
00:06:58,878 --> 00:07:03,323
Higher forms are about not writing the same title on two shelves.

120
00:07:03,323 --> 00:07:05,115
Do not skip the slot rule.

121
00:07:05,115 --> 00:07:08,740
Lists in cells make every later form meaningless.

122
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:17,198
Practice: find a column that needs a split, write the child table, migrate the values to rows, drop the list column.

123
00:07:17,198 --> 00:07:20,781
That migration is first normal form as a verb.

124
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:24,420
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

125
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:27,476
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

126
00:07:27,476 --> 00:07:29,059
What does first require?

127
00:07:29,059 --> 00:07:31,767
A key, types, atomic cells.

128
00:07:31,767 --> 00:07:32,990
Lists in a cell?

129
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:34,156
Not first.

130
00:07:34,156 --> 00:07:35,476
Split to rows.

131
00:07:35,476 --> 00:07:37,142
Repeating group columns?

132
00:07:37,142 --> 00:07:38,406
Also a list.

133
00:07:38,406 --> 00:07:39,281
Split.

134
00:07:39,281 --> 00:07:40,795
No primary key?

135
00:07:40,795 --> 00:07:41,962
Not first.

136
00:07:41,962 --> 00:07:43,170
Why start here?

137
00:07:43,170 --> 00:07:44,920
Later forms need a grain.

138
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:47,295
Comma separated product ids?

139
00:07:47,295 --> 00:07:48,295
A list.

140
00:07:48,295 --> 00:07:49,503
Child table.

141
00:07:49,503 --> 00:07:51,601
Is renaming tables first?

142
00:07:51,601 --> 00:07:52,365
No.

143
00:07:52,365 --> 00:07:54,406
What is the library metaphor?

144
00:07:54,406 --> 00:07:58,017
One book, one slot, labels on shelves.

145
00:07:58,017 --> 00:07:59,295
After first?

146
00:07:59,295 --> 00:08:02,712
Redundancy across key parts is second's job.

147
00:08:02,712 --> 00:08:04,434
Migration as a verb?

148
00:08:04,434 --> 00:08:08,476
Split the list, load rows, drop the list column.

149
00:08:08,476 --> 00:08:13,198
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

